WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.246
Approximation theory offers two complementary kinds
of statements.

00:00:04.326 --> 00:00:05.884
Forward, or Jackson-type,

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:11.194
theorems tell you how well a model can approximate
a sufficiently regular target.

00:00:11.274 --> 00:00:12.963
Inverse, or Bernstein-type,

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:18.600
theorems run the other direction: they assume a
target can be efficiently approximated

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.074
and then deduce what regularity the target must have.

00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:28.366
Inverse theorems are precisely the tool for
exposing fundamental limitations of an architecture.

00:00:28.446 --> 00:00:31.643
Earlier work proved such a result for linear RNNs,

00:00:31.723 --> 00:00:37.739
showing that efficiently approximable linear
targets must have exponentially decaying memory.

00:00:37.819 --> 00:00:41.278
The pressing question was whether adding nonlinearity,

00:00:41.358 --> 00:00:46.718
which greatly increases model capacity, would break
this so-called curse of memory.

00:00:46.798 --> 00:00:49.340
This paper was motivated to settle that.
